<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>How roof squares work</h2>
<p>A roofing square is 100 square feet of roof surface. Because roofs slope, the surface is larger than the footprint. The pitch multiplier converts one into the other: a 5/12 roof has about 8% more surface than its footprint, a 12/12 roof about 41% more.</p>
<h2>Tile versus shingle</h2>
<p>Standard concrete tile runs roughly 85 to 95 field pieces per square, depending on profile; composition shingles usually come three bundles to a square. Tile roofs also need underlayment, battens, hip and ridge pieces and bird stops that are priced separately. The material counts here are approximate.</p>
<h2>What to compare</h2>
<p>If the estimate’s squares are well below the calculator’s figure, check whether a slope, a porch roof or a garage was left out, and whether waste was applied. Then read about <a href="/disputed-claim-services/roof-damage-appraisal/">roof damage appraisal</a>.</p>

<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Is this as accurate as a measurement report?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. It is a quick check. Aerial reports and field measurements are more precise, especially on complex roofs.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why does waste differ by roof shape?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Hips, valleys and cut-up sections require more cutting, so more material is wasted.</p></div></details></div>
